PART IV — COMPLETE STATUTORY AND REGULATORY FRAMEWORK

5. Governing Legal Architecture

The statutory framework establishes the legal boundaries within which the +1HP accountability architecture operates.

It distinguishes federal constitutional protections, federal statutes, state statutes, judicial rules, administrative regulations, and institutional policies.

Critical rule: A statute is not automatically applicable merely because its subject matter resembles an alleged event. Applicability must be established through jurisdiction, facts, statutory elements, and the responsible institution.

5.1 Federal constitutional foundation

Authority	Governing subject	+1HP application

First Amendment	Speech, petition, assembly, and related protections	Public participation and institutional grievances.
Fourth Amendment	Search and seizure	Examination of documented searches, arrests, and property seizures.
Fifth Amendment	Federal due process and other protections	Federal institutional conduct and applicable due-process questions.
Sixth Amendment	Criminal procedural rights	Counsel, notice, confrontation, and fair-trial protections where applicable.
Eighth Amendment	Excessive bail, fines, and cruel and unusual punishment	Examination of documented penalties and correctional conditions.
Fourteenth Amendment	State due process and equal protection	State judicial proceedings, identification, and procedural fairness.


These constitutional provisions provide the foundational legal reference points. Their application depends on the particular proceeding and factual circumstances.

